WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.730
A prediction export
stores a fitted function.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.350
An unfinished training run must also
identify its exact next computation.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.080
Our separate demonstration uses the
existing forty five feature linear trainer

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.000
with an intercept and Adam.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.290
It saves parameters and
the training fitted scaler,

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.490
both optimizer moments and update index,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.690
the current shuffled order,
cursor and generator state,

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.290
processed examples,
schedule and monitoring.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.690
Source, data, schema,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:39.010
configuration and runtime
identities form its context.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.590
The run has a fixed three pass budget
and no validation model selection.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.320
Its purpose is continuation parity,

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.990
while the selected group
predictor stays unchanged.

00:00:53.335 --> 00:00:58.565
One pass contains eight thousand,
six hundred and forty five training rows.

00:00:59.135 --> 00:01:00.705
With batch size thirty two,

00:01:01.015 --> 00:01:06.445
it takes two hundred and seventy full
batches followed by a tail of five.

00:01:07.015 --> 00:01:12.435
Two further batches put update two
hundred and seventy three at cursor sixty

00:01:12.435 --> 00:01:14.765
four of the next permutation.

00:01:15.355 --> 00:01:18.665
The processed count is eight thousand,
seven hundred and nine.

00:01:19.815 --> 00:01:25.055
The inverse time schedule therefore gives
the next rate near zero point zero zero

00:01:25.055 --> 00:01:26.885
four nine eight two.

00:01:27.575 --> 00:01:30.025
Resetting the schedule
would change that move.

00:01:30.855 --> 00:01:34.305
The saved current order and
generator state also matter;

00:01:34.755 --> 00:01:38.145
restarting the seed alone does
not identify this position.

00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:44.222
The loader first finds one
complete compatible generation.

00:01:44.832 --> 00:01:48.722
The worker verifies the frozen training
data and current source context,

00:01:49.112 --> 00:01:51.642
then reuses the saved means and scales.

00:01:51.952 --> 00:01:54.602
It does not fit a new
transform during resume.

00:01:55.572 --> 00:01:57.982
The same trainer receives
the restored state,

00:01:58.212 --> 00:02:02.172
so the update algorithm stays shared
between the uninterrupted and resumed

00:02:02.172 --> 00:02:02.882
paths.

00:02:03.232 --> 00:02:06.342
The next batch has thirty
two training row positions,

00:02:06.772 --> 00:02:08.922
beginning three thousand,
one hundred and fifty six,

00:02:09.452 --> 00:02:11.242
then three thousand,
two hundred and fifty.

00:02:11.852 --> 00:02:13.002
These are positions,

00:02:13.472 --> 00:02:14.902
not raw record IDs;

00:02:15.452 --> 00:02:19.422
reordering the data would change their
meaning and must fail the context check.

00:02:21.503 --> 00:02:25.773
Inspect the hour eight coefficient
in standardized feature coordinates.

00:02:26.323 --> 00:02:30.393
The next batch gives gradient negative
fifty four point zero six four.

00:02:30.863 --> 00:02:34.573
The saved first moment is negative
thirty point five seven six,

00:02:34.963 --> 00:02:39.293
and the saved second moment is about four
hundred and fifty nine point six one.

00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:43.173
Apply Adam decay and add the
new gradient contributions.

00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:48.663
The next moments become negative thirty
two point nine two four and four hundred

00:02:48.663 --> 00:02:50.833
and sixty two point zero seven three.

00:02:51.263 --> 00:02:54.953
Bias correction still uses the retained
index, now two hundred and seventy four.

00:02:56.223 --> 00:02:59.553
A weights only export contains
none of this gradient history.

00:03:01.817 --> 00:03:06.172
Correct the two moments by their
respective decay factors at update two

00:03:06.172 --> 00:03:06.707
hundred and seventy four.

00:03:08.357 --> 00:03:11.209
The corrected second moment
is about one thousand,

00:03:11.209 --> 00:03:14.307
nine hundred and twenty
seven point one three four.

00:03:14.737 --> 00:03:20.467
Divide the corrected first moment
by its square root plus epsilon,

00:03:20.677 --> 00:03:22.747
then multiply by the scheduled rate.

00:03:23.697 --> 00:03:27.940
Subtracting this negative direction raises
the weight from one point one nine four

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:33.087
one five four to one point one
nine seven eight nine zero.

00:03:33.497 --> 00:03:37.747
The independent audit recomputes the
batch gradient and this arithmetic.

00:03:38.437 --> 00:03:42.467
In another batch, the latest gradient
can disagree with moment history,

00:03:42.697 --> 00:03:46.147
so its sign alone is not
Adam’s complete update rule.

00:03:48.192 --> 00:03:52.898
The restarted run reaches eight hundred
and thirteen updates and twenty five

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:53.241
thousand,

00:03:53.241 --> 00:03:58.662
nine hundred and thirty five examples
with the exact uninterrupted parameters,

00:03:58.992 --> 00:03:59.662
moments,

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:01.702
order state and schedule.

00:04:02.452 --> 00:04:06.602
Its next batch and common
monitoring observations also match.

00:04:07.232 --> 00:04:08.822
Wall clock fields are excluded.

00:04:09.572 --> 00:04:14.102
Now zero the moments at the
pause while keeping parameters,

00:04:14.452 --> 00:04:17.082
index, remaining order and budget.

00:04:17.852 --> 00:04:22.342
Final parameters differ by up
to one point zero zero two nine.

00:04:23.072 --> 00:04:25.702
The missing history
changes the computation.

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:30.942
Exact recovery does not imply
convergence or strong quality:

00:04:31.692 --> 00:04:35.232
this short run’s training half
squared error is about seven thousand,

00:04:35.232 --> 00:04:36.762
seven hundred and eighty two.

